  "interpretation": {
    "directional_surroundings": "The directional bounded-speed excess seen in original/high-pass (p around 0.09 to 0.11 against geometry surrogate) disappears after phase randomization (p around 0.64). Propagation-like geometry is therefore not robust under the spectral surrogate, even though aggregate coherence survives.",
    "method_frame": "This supports the generic anomaly-plus-surroundings workflow: surface anomalous entities, inspect surrounding dimensions such as time, space, frequency, phase, velocity, period, region, and graph recurrence, then mask known structures and rerun on residuals.",
    "next": "Build a known-structure masking pass rather than declaring novelty: identify dominant spectral/swell modes, subtract or condition on them, then rerun detector and surroundings audits on residual structure.",
    "region_counting": "Raw mixed-region delta is mostly pair/window exposure. Pair-normalized original/high-pass same-region and mixed-region deltas are nearly equal, so cross-region dominance alone should not be treated as physical evidence.",
    "spectral_surrogate": "High-pass phase-randomized windows preserve each buoy/window power spectrum while breaking phase/time structure. Aggregate coherence did not collapse; delta increased from 3550.6640 to 3798.9173. This means the agnostic system still finds organization when original phase relationships are destroyed. It may be recovering score/spectral-distribution structure rather than propagation timing.",
    "tide": "The 30-minute high-pass control leaves aggregate delta and z effect size essentially unchanged, so tide/slow forcing is not carrying the result."
